    OpenSearch Dashboards experiences severe performance degradation due to continuous polling of GET */_mapping requests when the "Automatically refresh autocomplete suggestions" feature is enabled in Dev Tools.

    Observed Behavior:

    - Continuous GET */_mapping requests are sent through /dashboards/api/console/proxy when autocomplete refresh is enabled in devtools
    - Dashboard backend process expands heap significantly to process large mapping responses
    - In severe cases, the dashboard backend becomes completely unavailable
    - Performance issues persist even after cluster upgrades (observed from OS 2.17 to 2.19)

    Impact:

    - Production environments with large indices become unusable
    - Users forced to disable autocomplete functionality as workaround
    - Performance regression compared to Elasticsearch/Kibana behavior

    Evidence:

    - CloudWatch metrics show HeapUsed spikes correlating with GET */_mapping requests in ELB logs
    - Elasticsearch domains with similar mapping sizes handle the same requests without performance issues
    - Disabling "Automatically refresh autocomplete suggestions" resolves the performance problem

    Current Workarounds:

    - Disabling "Automatically refresh autocomplete suggestions" (reduces functionality)

    **Additional context**

    Environment Details:

    - OpenSearch Version: 2.17, 2.19 (issue persists across versions)
    - Migration Context: Performance regression observed after migrating from Elasticsearch to OpenSearch
    - Cluster Size: Large production clusters with extensive mapping data


    Comparison with Elasticsearch:

    - Elasticsearch/Kibana handles identical mapping requests without performance degradation
    - Kibana process remains stable even when large requests fail
    - OpenSearch Dashboards backend becomes completely unresponsive under same conditions

    Expected Outcome:

    - OpenSearch Dashboards should handle large mapping responses as efficiently as Elasticsearch/Kibana
    - Autocomplete functionality should not cause system-wide performance issues

